WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
Least privilege starts before permissions are granted.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:08.040
The team should be able to describe what the script is supposed to accomplish in plain operational terms.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:17.740
Is it reporting status, collecting diagnostics, publishing an artifact, restarting one service, copying approved files, or preparing a deployment directory?

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.440
A script with a vague purpose tends to receive vague access.

5
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.140
A script with a clear job can be reviewed against that job.

6
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.100
The next step is to identify what the script actually touches.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:38.800
List the systems it connects to, the files it reads, the directories it writes, the commands it calls, the data it handles, and the expected outputs it produces.

8
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:48.020
This does not need to be formal architecture documentation for every small script, but it should be clear enough that a maintainer can understand why each permission exists.

9
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:54.440
Permissions should be tied to workflow needs, not granted because a broad account happened to make the first test pass.

10
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.960
Read, write, execute, delete, and administer are different capabilities.

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.380
A script that reads status should not automatically be able to change state.

12
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.800
A script that writes to one output directory should not automatically be able to write into configuration directories.

13
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:18.140
A script that starts one approved helper should not automatically be able to administer the host.

14
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:26.640
Separating these capabilities helps reviewers grant the minimum access required and notice when a requested permission does not match the intended outcome.

15
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.840
It is equally useful to document disallowed actions.

16
00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:42.300
If a script should never touch production, never delete inputs, never modify source directories, never use production credentials, or never run outside a maintenance window, say so.

17
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.000
Ownership also matters.

18
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:51.060
Someone should know who maintains the script, who approves permission changes, and who reviews access after the workflow changes.

19
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:57.080
Least privilege becomes sustainable when permission decisions are connected to purpose, ownership, and review.
